  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian Assistance to IDPs and Other Conflict-Affected People in Lebanon</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the outbreak of hostilities in the Occupied Palestinian Territory of Gaza on 7 October 2023, cross-border clashes between Israel and Hezbollah have intensified daily, causing military and civilian casualties on both sides. These clashes have increased in intensity and scope, with the affected steadily expanding. Strikes now target sites far beyond the Blue Line, including in Baalbek, Saida, Jezzine and the Bekaa. The situation is tense and could escalate beyond the south at any moment, similar to the 2006 war between Hezbollah and Israel. Since October 2023, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) in Lebanon has been addressing the humanitarian needs of internally displaced persons (IDPs) and supporting the Government of Lebanon to prepare for a possible escalation of conflict. IOM&#x2019;s efforts focus on Displacement Tracking Matrix activities, site coordination, health assistance, protection services and the distribution of essential relief items to IDPs and host families, however needs remain high. Building on its existing programmes and existing field presence in Southern Lebanon and coordination structures, IOM is providing a comprehensive and integrated humanitarian intervention with the main objective of ensuring dignified, safe, and secure living conditions and basic services for vulnerable populations affected by conflict in Southern Lebanon.</narrative>
  </description>
